WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.183 --> 00:00:11.743
One dollar and eighty-seven cents.

2
00:00:12.024 --> 00:00:12.644
That was all.

3
00:00:13.044 --> 00:00:14.824
And sixty cents of it was in pennies.

4
00:00:15.505 --> 00:00:19.926
These pennies were saved by Della, bargaining with the grocer and butcher until she blushed.

5
00:00:20.606 --> 00:00:22.367
Three times a day Della counted it.

6
00:00:23.047 --> 00:00:24.687
One dollar and eighty-seven cents.

7
00:00:25.127 --> 00:00:26.508
And the next day would be Christmas.

8
00:00:27.456 --> 00:00:30.898
There was clearly nothing to do but flop down on the shabby brown couch.

9
00:00:31.638 --> 00:00:34.640
Della sobbed, but eventually her sobbing subsided.

10
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.022
She looked around at their apartment: a furnished flat for eight dollars a week.

11
00:00:39.723 --> 00:00:43.005
It was not exactly a pauper's flat, but it came close.

12
00:00:43.605 --> 00:00:47.947
There was a letterbox into which no letter would go, and a doorbell that made no sound.

13
00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:50.869
On the door was a sign, "Mr.

14
00:00:50.929 --> 00:00:52.169
James Dillingham Young."

15
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:53.550
But whenever Mr.

16
00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:57.857
James Dillingham Young came home, he was called Jim and hugged by Mrs.

17
00:00:57.917 --> 00:01:01.579
James Dillingham Young, already introduced to you as Della.

18
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:03.120
Della finished crying.

19
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:08.004
Tomorrow would be Christmas Day and she only had a dollar and eighty-seven cents with which to buy

20
00:01:08.064 --> 00:01:08.744
Jim a present.

21
00:01:09.304 --> 00:01:11.486
She had been saving every penny she could for months.

22
00:01:12.046 --> 00:01:16.950
Twenty dollars a week doesn't go far, and living expenses had been greater than she had calculated.

23
00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.212
Della stared at herself in the long glass windows.

24
00:01:20.792 --> 00:01:22.133
Suddenly she whirled around.

25
00:01:22.654 --> 00:01:26.056
Her eyes were shining but her face had lost its color in twenty seconds.

26
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.260
Rapidly she let down her hair.

27
00:01:28.981 --> 00:01:32.702
Della's beautiful hair rippled and shined like a cascade of brown waters.

28
00:01:33.302 --> 00:01:34.462
It reached below her knee.

29
00:01:35.082 --> 00:01:36.183
Della had an idea.

30
00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:42.344
Maybe she could afford a Christmas present Now there were two things that Jim and Della Dillingham Young

31
00:01:42.484 --> 00:01:47.586
adored. One was Jim's gold watch that had been his father's and his grandfather's.

32
00:01:48.106 --> 00:01:49.306
The other was Della's hair.

33
00:01:50.426 --> 00:01:52.547
Della put her hair up quickly and nervously.

34
00:01:53.207 --> 00:01:56.188
She put on her old brown jacket and her old brown hat.

35
00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.824
With a whirl of her skirt and the brilliant sparkle in her eyes, she fluttered out the door

36
00:02:02.224 --> 00:02:03.726
and down the stairs to the street.

37
00:02:04.306 --> 00:02:09.090
Della walked downtown and stopped outside a shop whose sign said, "Mrs.

38
00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.752
Sofronie. Hair Goods of All Kinds."

39
00:02:12.413 --> 00:02:14.394
She took a deep breath and went inside.

40
00:02:14.414 --> 00:02:16.336
"Will you buy my hair?"

41
00:02:16.816 --> 00:02:18.978
Della asked a large lady at the counter.

42
00:02:18.998 --> 00:02:23.782
"Take off your hat and let's have a look at it," the lady said.

43
00:02:24.642 --> 00:02:25.223
She was Mrs.

44
00:02:25.263 --> 00:02:26.544
Sofronie, the shopkeeper.

45
00:02:27.052 --> 00:02:28.453
The cascade rippled down.

46
00:02:29.113 --> 00:02:31.114
"Twenty dollars," said Mrs.

47
00:02:31.154 --> 00:02:34.056
Sofronie, lifting the mass of hair with a practiced hand.

48
00:02:34.076 --> 00:02:36.717
"Give it to me quick," said Della.

49
00:02:37.518 --> 00:02:40.600
Della spent the next two hours looking in stores for Jim's present.

50
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.201
She found it at last.

51
00:02:42.661 --> 00:02:44.822
It surely had been made for Jim and no one else.

52
00:02:45.282 --> 00:02:49.405
There was nothing like it in any of the stores, and she had turned all of them inside

53
00:02:49.525 --> 00:02:52.907
out. It was a platinum fob chain with a simple design.

54
00:02:53.487 --> 00:02:58.521
It was not gaudy or too ornamental, but its simplicity and style demonstrated its quality.

55
00:02:59.142 --> 00:03:02.083
As soon as she saw it, she knew that it must be Jim's.

56
00:03:03.083 --> 00:03:06.865
Twenty-one dollars it cost, and she hurried home with the eighty-seven cents.

57
00:03:07.665 --> 00:03:11.467
With the fob chain on his watch, Jim could check the time in anyone's company.

58
00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:17.610
Grand as the watch was, sometimes Jim checked it secretly because he was ashamed of the old leather

59
00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.650
strap that he used in the place of a chain.

60
00:03:20.331 --> 00:03:25.277
When Della reached home, she got out her curling irons, lit the gas and went to work on

61
00:03:25.317 --> 00:03:26.697
her now very short hair.

62
00:03:27.398 --> 00:03:32.360
Within forty minutes her head was covered with tiny, close-lying curls that made her look like a schoolboy.

63
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.641
She looked at her reflection in the mirror carefully and critically.

64
00:03:35.661 --> 00:03:39.303
"Jim will say I look like a Coney Island chorus girl!

65
00:03:39.843 --> 00:03:40.883
But what could I do?

66
00:03:41.363 --> 00:03:44.385
What could I buy with a dollar and eighty-seven cents?"

67
00:03:44.985 --> 00:03:49.287
At seven o'clock the coffee was made and the frying-pan was on the back of the stove and

68
00:03:49.327 --> 00:03:50.407
ready to cook the chops.

69
00:03:51.067 --> 00:03:52.048
Jim was never late.

70
00:03:52.576 --> 00:03:56.239
Della doubled the fob chain in her hand and sat on the corner of the table near the

71
00:03:56.279 --> 00:03:57.680
door that he always entered.

72
00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.662
Then Della heard Jim's steps on the stairs.

73
00:04:01.122 --> 00:04:02.283
She turned white for a moment.

74
00:04:02.863 --> 00:04:06.926
Della had a habit of saying a little silent prayer about the simplest everyday things.

75
00:04:07.426 --> 00:04:11.669
Now she whispered, "Please, God, make him think that I am still pretty."

76
00:04:12.049 --> 00:04:14.611
The door opened, and Jim stepped in and closed it.

77
00:04:15.131 --> 00:04:16.472
He looked thin and serious.

78
00:04:17.053 --> 00:04:18.974
Poor fellow, he was only twenty-two.

79
00:04:19.494 --> 00:04:21.876
He needed a new overcoat and he had no gloves.

80
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.264
Jim's eyes fixed upon Della, and there was an expression in them she could not read, and it

81
00:04:27.324 --> 00:04:28.025
terrified her.

82
00:04:28.645 --> 00:04:34.069
It was not anger, nor surprise, nor disapproval, nor horror, nor any of the sentiments she had been

83
00:04:34.109 --> 00:04:34.750
prepared for.

84
00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.293
He simply stared at her with a peculiar expression on his face.

85
00:04:38.973 --> 00:04:41.195
Della jumped off the table and went to him.

86
00:04:41.215 --> 00:04:43.277
"Jim, darling," she cried.

87
00:04:43.297 --> 00:04:44.698
"Don't look at me that way.

88
00:04:44.718 --> 00:04:50.062
I cut my hair off because I couldn't have lived through another Christmas without giving you a present.

89
00:04:50.703 --> 00:04:51.704
It will grow again.

90
00:04:52.352 --> 00:04:53.552
You don't mind, do you?

91
00:04:54.273 --> 00:04:56.793
Say Merry Christmas, Jim, and let's be happy!"

92
00:04:57.433 --> 00:04:59.534
Jim's strange expression had not changed.

93
00:04:59.934 --> 00:05:02.255
Della pleaded with him, "Come on, Jim.

94
00:05:02.315 --> 00:05:04.215
I bought you such a beautiful gift."

95
00:05:04.675 --> 00:05:09.897
"You've cut off your hair," said Jim slowly, as if it was taking him a long time to

96
00:05:09.937 --> 00:05:11.277
understand this simple fact.

97
00:05:11.297 --> 00:05:13.218
"I cut it off and sold it.

98
00:05:13.798 --> 00:05:15.958
But I'm still the same without my hair!"

99
00:05:15.978 --> 00:05:18.399
"Where do you say your hair is gone?"

100
00:05:19.019 --> 00:05:19.439
he said.

101
00:05:19.459 --> 00:05:21.320
"I told you, I sold it."

102
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.321
Jim suddenly blinked his eyes and shook his head.

103
00:05:24.721 --> 00:05:28.183
Then he drew a package from his overcoat pocket and threw it upon the table.

104
00:05:28.203 --> 00:05:33.465
"Don't make any mistake, Dell," Jim said.

105
00:05:33.485 --> 00:05:36.387
"There's no way I can love you any less.

106
00:05:36.967 --> 00:05:39.848
No haircut or shampoo can change the way I feel about you.

107
00:05:40.549 --> 00:05:44.951
But if you unwrap that package you'll see why I've been acting strange these past five minutes."

108
00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:47.932
Dell's nimble fingers tore at the string and paper.

109
00:05:48.504 --> 00:05:52.006
There was an ecstatic scream of joy and then there was a wailing of tears.

110
00:05:52.647 --> 00:05:58.931
Upon the table there was a set of hair combs─beautiful combs made from real tortoise shells with jewels

111
00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:02.373
on the rims─just the shade to wear in her nonexistent hair.

112
00:06:02.954 --> 00:06:04.755
Dell knew they were expensive hair combs.

113
00:06:05.235 --> 00:06:08.958
She had wished for them but thought she would never own such beautiful things.

114
00:06:09.658 --> 00:06:12.840
And now they were hers, but she had no hair to wear them in!

115
00:06:13.821 --> 00:06:19.276
But she hugged them to her chest, and she looked up with a smile and said, "My hair

116
00:06:19.356 --> 00:06:20.777
grows so fast, Jim!"

117
00:06:21.477 --> 00:06:23.618
Jim had not yet seen his beautiful present.

118
00:06:24.338 --> 00:06:27.579
Della held out the fob chain eagerly upon her open palm.

119
00:06:27.599 --> 00:06:29.199
"Isn't it smart, Jim?

120
00:06:29.779 --> 00:06:31.640
I hunted all over town to find it.

121
00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.781
You'll have to check the time a hundred times a day now.

122
00:06:35.481 --> 00:06:36.421
Give me your watch.

123
00:06:36.842 --> 00:06:38.262
I want to see how it looks.

124
00:06:38.982 --> 00:06:43.003
I know it will look a hundred times nicer than that old leather strap!"

125
00:06:43.624 --> 00:06:48.669
Instead of obeying, Jim flopped down onto the couch and put his hands under the back of his

126
00:06:48.709 --> 00:06:49.509
head and smiled.

127
00:06:49.529 --> 00:06:55.971
"Della," he said, "let's put our Christmas presents away and keep them for a while.

128
00:06:56.591 --> 00:06:58.192
They're too nice to use just now.

129
00:06:58.952 --> 00:07:01.433
I sold the watch to get the money to buy your combs.

130
00:07:02.473 --> 00:07:03.293
Let's have some dinner."

131
00:07:04.353 --> 00:07:08.554
The Magi were three wise men who brought valuable gifts to baby Jesus in the manger.

132
00:07:09.375 --> 00:07:12.616
Perhaps the Magi were the first people to ever give Christmas presents.

133
00:07:13.336 --> 00:07:15.889
In this story, Christmas gifts were very important.

134
00:07:16.729 --> 00:07:21.470
Della and Jim loved each other so much that they were willing to sacrifice the greatest treasures in

135
00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.251
their house─Della's hair and Jim's watch.

136
00:07:24.951 --> 00:07:27.872
They didn't have a lot of money, but they had a lot of love.

137
00:07:28.532 --> 00:07:35.615
Perhaps they were foolish, but Della and Jim understood about special gifts and could enjoy Christmas together.
